{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791574946,"nonce":"bugYd9f6fC/86MUn3L9+FbsvMSc=","message":"Fixed for signal-bound writes, both render properties and layout style. Option B as chosen: a state rule is a layer over the node's own properties.\n\nHow: a bound write now carries the same change a second time (PartialPropertyUpdate.beneath_write). The drain applies it to the properties the node returns to (base_styles, base_taffy_styles), then puts the state rules that are applying back on top, so a rule that sets the same property keeps winning and the written value is what the node returns to. The tree records the state styles applied to each node (state_layers) at the points the passes apply them, and drops them where base_styles is dropped. Tests: blinc_layout/tests/state_layers.rs, 13, for the class and id paths, a change before the first hover, between hovers and during one, and a rule that sets the written property; each of the three halves fails when removed.\n\nWider than first filed: any node with an id is reset by the id path whenever the sheet has any complex state rule (has_state_styles), so in a sheet like cn's most nodes with ids were exposed, not only nodes a rule matched.\n\nNot covered: writers that bypass the bound path (queue_prop_update_partial called directly, animation tickers, stateful prop replacement) carry no beneath write, so they behave as before. The snapshot remains the physical store of what is underneath rather than being replaced by per-property tier slots (7cabafe).\n\nCommit: 00cc32454a88bd770cf994c70793032a5fe85369","files":null}]}